
WARNING! pinctrl set writes directly to the IO MUX and GPIO registers
ignoring whatever else may be using them (such as Linux drivers) -
it is designed as a debug tool, only use it if you know what you
are doing and at your own risk! A write can race with the
read-modify-write of the kernel pin driver.

Running pinctrl with the help argument prints this help.
pinctrl can get and print the state of a GPIO (or all GPIOs)
and can be used to set the function, pulls and value of a GPIO.
pinctrl must be run as root.
Use:
  pinctrl [-p] [-v] get [GPIO]
OR
  pinctrl [-p] [-v] [-e] [-f] set <GPIO> [options]
OR
  pinctrl [-p] [-v] poll <GPIO>
OR
  pinctrl [-p] [-v] funcs [GPIO]
OR
  pinctrl [-p] [-v] lev [GPIO]
OR
  pinctrl dump > regs.txt

GPIO is a comma-separated list of GPIO names, numbers or ranges (without
spaces), e.g. 4 or 8-19 or PA_CTRL,50-51

Note that omitting [GPIO] from "pinctrl get" prints all GPIOs.
If the -p option is given, GPIO numbers are replaced by pin numbers on the
board: header J2 on the Function-CoreBoard-1, the pads of the
ESP32-S31-WROOM-3 module on the module alone and on the Korvo-1.
If the -v option is given, the output also has the drive strength and
open drain. Including the -e option in a "set" causes pinctrl to echo back
the new pin states.
pinctrl funcs will dump all the possible GPIO alt functions in CSV format
or if [GPIO] is specified the alternate funcs just for that specific GPIO.
pinctrl dump writes the registers and the board to a snapshot, and
--regs FILE reads the snapshot instead of the hardware.

pinctrl set refuses the flash pins, the pins in gpio-reserved-ranges of the
device tree, and the lines that a kernel driver uses (-f overrides the
last). It warns before it changes a strapping pin (36, 37, 60, 61).

Valid [options] for pinctrl set are:
  ip      set GPIO as input
  op      set GPIO as output
  a0-a4   set GPIO to IO MUX function 0 to 4 (a1 is the GPIO matrix)
  no      set GPIO to no function (NONE)
  pu      set GPIO in-pad pull up
  pd      set GPIO in-pad pull down
  pn      set GPIO pull none (no pull)
  dh      set GPIO to drive high (1) level (only valid if set to be an output)
  dl      set GPIO to drive low (0) level (only valid if set to be an output)
Examples:
  pinctrl get              Prints state of all GPIOs one per line
  pinctrl get 10           Prints state of GPIO10
  pinctrl get 10,11        Prints state of GPIO10 and GPIO11
  pinctrl set 47 a3        Set GPIO47 to fsel 3 function (CAM_DATA1_IN)
  pinctrl -e set 47 pu     Enable GPIO47 in-pad pull up, echoing the result
  pinctrl set 47 op        Set GPIO47 to be an output
  pinctrl set 47 dl        Set GPIO47 to output low/zero (must already be set as an output)
  pinctrl set 47 ip pd     Set GPIO47 to input with pull down
  pinctrl set 47 op pn dh  Set GPIO47 to output with no pull and driving high
  pinctrl lev 47           Prints the level (1 or 0) of GPIO47
rc=0
